{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-108911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}+y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}$ $$\n- ${y}^{′}-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x={y}^{6}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\sqrt{x},\\,q(x)={x}^{3},\\,n=-1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,q(x)=-\\sqrt{x},\\,n=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e92c179223f364cc17e57f2e068cfbdbc352282c0996c6e9f9b0804a8b15d968"} {"question" "q-108911" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108911" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108911" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-{e}^{x}{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{y}$\n- ${y}^{3}{y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\sqrt{y}$\n- $y\\,{y}^{′}+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y={cos}^{2}⁡y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y={y}^{-5/2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation.  If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+x(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{y})=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡y}{y}.\n$$\n\nIt is missing both the $p(x)y$ and $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e92c179223f364cc17e57f2e068cfbdbc352282c0996c6e9f9b0804a8b15d968"} {"question" "q-108918" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108918" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the given equations is a Bernoulli differential equation"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108918" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{-2}{y}^{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\n- ${y}^{′}-{e}^{x}y={x}^{2}{y}^{5}$\n- ${y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-x{y}^{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing both the $p(x)y$ and $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-{e}^{x},\\,q(x)={x}^{2},\\,n=5.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e92c179223f364cc17e57f2e068cfbdbc352282c0996c6e9f9b0804a8b15d968"} {"question" "q-108913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None of the given equations is a Bernoulli differential equation"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108916" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{2}{y}^{′}-x{y}^{5}={y}^{4}$\n- $y{y}^{′}-x\\sqrt{y}={x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108916" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-x{y}^{3}={y}^{2}\n$$\n\nand this is not a Bernoulli differential equation because it is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. If we attempt to write the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\n$$\n\nand this is not a Bernoulli differential equation because it is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x){y}^{n}$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the given equations is a Bernoulli differential equation.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108916" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e92c179223f364cc17e57f2e068cfbdbc352282c0996c6e9f9b0804a8b15d968"} {"question" "q-108916" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108916" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None of the given equations is a Bernoulli differential equation"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108916" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of the given equations is a Bernoulli differential equation"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+\\sqrt{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=x{y}^{3}$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\n- Equation III is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2},\\,q(x)=\\sqrt{x},\\,n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e92c179223f364cc17e57f2e068cfbdbc352282c0996c6e9f9b0804a8b15d968"} {"question" "q-108914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the given equations is a Bernoulli differential equation"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-108914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+3xy=-{x}^{4}{y}^{3}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=3x,\\,q(x)=-{x}^{4},\\,n=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}\\frac{dy}{dx}+3x{y}^{-2}=-{x}^{4}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3xz=-{x}^{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-6xz=2{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-6x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e92c179223f364cc17e57f2e068cfbdbc352282c0996c6e9f9b0804a8b15d968"} {"question" "q-109014" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109014" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3x"] ["math" "-6x"] ["math" "-9x"] ["math" "-\\frac{x}{9}"] ["math" "3x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109014" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109009" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x={y}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109009" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,q(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,n=5.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{5},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-5}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{-4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-4{y}^{-5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-5}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+4z\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109009" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e92c179223f364cc17e57f2e068cfbdbc352282c0996c6e9f9b0804a8b15d968"} {"question" "q-109009" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109009" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109009" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{y}{x}=x\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{{y}^{1/2}}{x}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\,z=x\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{2x}\\,z=\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=\\frac{x}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e92c179223f364cc17e57f2e068cfbdbc352282c0996c6e9f9b0804a8b15d968"} {"question" "q-106070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3x}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2x}"] ["math" "\\frac{x}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-y(2x+3{y}^{2})=0\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-y(2x+3{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-2xy-3{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{dx}-2xy & =3{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}-2{x}^{-1}y & =3{x}^{-2}{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-2{x}^{-1},\\,q(x)=3{x}^{-2},\\,n=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}\\frac{dy}{dx}-2{x}^{-1}{y}^{-2}=3{x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2{x}^{-1}z=3{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+4{x}^{-1}z=-6{x}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=-6{x}^{-2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e92c179223f364cc17e57f2e068cfbdbc352282c0996c6e9f9b0804a8b15d968"} {"question" "q-106071" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106071" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3{x}^{-1}"] ["math" "-6{x}^{-1}"] ["math" "-6{x}^{-2}"] ["math" "2{x}^{-2}"] ["math" "4{x}^{-1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106071" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6{x}^{-2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y=x{y}^{4}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-4}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}{y}^{-3}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-3{y}^{-4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}z=x\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2}z=-3x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-3{x}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e92c179223f364cc17e57f2e068cfbdbc352282c0996c6e9f9b0804a8b15d968"} {"question" "q-106069" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106069" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3x"] ["math" "-3{x}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{x}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{x}{3}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106069" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3{x}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}-xy.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{-1/2}-xy \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy & ={y}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}y & ={x}^{-2}{y}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{-1},\\,q(x)={x}^{-2},\\,n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}{y}^{3/2}={x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{-1}z={x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1}z=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{3}{2x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/2x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{(3/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{3/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{3/2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2}) \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}z & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2}z) & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{3/2}z & =3{x}^{1/2}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}+C}{{x}^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{3/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{3/2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}+C}{{x}^{3/2}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{{(3\\sqrt{x}+C)}^{2/3}}{x}\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e92c179223f364cc17e57f2e068cfbdbc352282c0996c6e9f9b0804a8b15d968"} {"question" "q-109177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2{x}^{2/3}-Cx"] ["math" "y=3\\sqrt{x}+C{x}^{2/3}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{(3+Cx)}^{2/3}}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{(3\\sqrt{x}+C)}^{2/3}}{x}"] ["math" "y={(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}+C}{x})}^{2/3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109177" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{{(3\\sqrt{x}+C)}^{2/3}}{x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109158" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xy.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109158" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xy \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & ={x}^{3}{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{{y}^{-1}}{x}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}z=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109158" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e92c179223f364cc17e57f2e068cfbdbc352282c0996c6e9f9b0804a8b15d968"} {"question" "q-109158" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109158" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{C+2{x}^{3}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x}{C-{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}+C}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{C-x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109158" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}+2xy}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}+2xy}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2{x}^{-1}y & ={x}^{-2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-2{x}^{-1},\\,q(x)={x}^{-2},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-2{x}^{-1}{y}^{-1}={x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2{x}^{-1}z={x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2{x}^{-1}z=-{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (-{x}^{-2}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{2}z & =-x+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-x}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-x}{{x}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{C-x}\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e92c179223f364cc17e57f2e068cfbdbc352282c0996c6e9f9b0804a8b15d968"} {"question" "q-109172" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109172" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{C-4x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{C-x}{2{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x+C}{{x}^{3}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{C-x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{Cx+2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109172" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{C-x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy=x{y}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=x,\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+x{y}^{-1}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+xz=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-xz=-x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-x,\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-xz) & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\cdot (-x) \\\\ {e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-xz{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} & =-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}) & =-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2})\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}+C \\\\ z & =1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e92c179223f364cc17e57f2e068cfbdbc352282c0996c6e9f9b0804a8b15d968"} {"question" "q-109026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{-{x}^{2}}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x}{1+C{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}}{C+x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-109026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y={y}^{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-1,\\,q(x)={e}^{x},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{-1}={e}^{x}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z=-{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=1,\\,Q(x)=-{e}^{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot (-{e}^{x}) \\\\ {e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x}) & =-{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{C-{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e92c179223f364cc17e57f2e068cfbdbc352282c0996c6e9f9b0804a8b15d968"} {"question" "q-106073" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106073" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{2x{e}^{x}}{C-2{e}^{2x}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{C-{e}^{2x}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{C-{e}^{x}}{2{e}^{x}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{C-2{e}^{x}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "q-106073" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{C-{e}^{2x}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/state.json" "file_sha256" "0478949aae93f2a0c01ed22b53b3b5ef62572c6b5257526d1fe694c385651eb2"} {"question" "e-9069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- $y\\,y' + x^2y^2 = e^x y^4$\n\n- $xy' + x^3y = -x^2 y^3$\n\n- $y' + y\\cos{x} = e^{y+1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/example-9069.json" "file_sha256" "cd182285cf184b3a2b4506ecdb8fe9a7f0e5803c6e4bc4050f68997a04718a24"} {"question" "e-9069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' + x^2y = e^x y^3\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) = e^x, \\qquad n=3.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\ny' + x^2y = -xy^3\n$$\n\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) =-x, \\qquad n=3.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x)y^n$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/example-9069.json" "file_sha256" "cd182285cf184b3a2b4506ecdb8fe9a7f0e5803c6e4bc4050f68997a04718a24"} {"question" "e-8963" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The substitution $z=y^m$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form $z'(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/example-8963.json" "file_sha256" "9c442fa5cddb9d41104d2f1a951caed5069f8bde59f3bb70bcf6598f6ff98e81"} {"question" "e-8963" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = x^3, \\qquad q(x) = 1, \\qquad n=-\\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by $y^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny^{1/2} \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y^{3/2}= 1.\\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = \\dfrac{3}{2}y^{1/2} \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny^{1/2}\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(\\ast)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^3\\,z = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{3}{2}x^3z = \\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/example-8963.json" "file_sha256" "9c442fa5cddb9d41104d2f1a951caed5069f8bde59f3bb70bcf6598f6ff98e81"} {"question" "e-8965" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{y}{x}  = \\dfrac{x^2}{y} .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/example-8965.json" "file_sha256" "84e8c059b1c7f564cad2389cd33d5af4c691a5e39a257bca6f67c170335e4f64"} {"question" "e-8965" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad q(x) = x^2, \\qquad n=-1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by $y^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + \\dfrac{y^{2}}{x}= x^2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{1}{x}z  = x^2\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{2}{x} z\n\t\t=2x^2\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=2{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (2{x}^{2}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =2{x}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2}) & =2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{x}^{2} & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}+C}{5{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5C_1.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z=y^{2}.$ This results in the general solution\n\n$$\ny^{ 2} =\\dfrac{2x^5 + C}{5x^2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043823/example-8965.json" "file_sha256" "84e8c059b1c7f564cad2389cd33d5af4c691a5e39a257bca6f67c170335e4f64"}] "usage" {"e-8963" [] "q-106070" [] "q-106069" [] "e-8965" [] "e-9069" [] "q-106073" []} "basis" 1631 "retractions" [[17592186147667 :question/problem "The substitution $z = y^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{y}{x} = x\\sqrt{y}$\ninto an equation of the form $z^{′}(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x)$. What is the function $Q(x)$?"] [17592186147666 :question/problem "The substitution $z = y^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + x^{2}y = xy^{4}$\ninto an equation of the form $z^{′}(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x)$. What is the function $P(x)$?"] [17592186147668 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} - y = y^{2}e^{x}$."] [17592186147334 :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n1. $y\\,y' + x^2y^2 = e^x y^4$\n2. $xy' + x^3y = -x^2 y^3$\n3. $y' + y\\cos{x} = e^{y+1}$"] [17592186147334 :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n$y' + x^2y = e^x y^3$\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n$p(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) = e^x, \\qquad n=3$.\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n$y' + x^2y = -xy^3$\nwhich is a Bernoulli differential equation with\n$p(x) = x^2, \\qquad q(x) =-x, \\qquad n=3$.\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $q(x)y^n$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186147331 :question/problem "The substitution $z=y^m$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}$\ninto an equation of the form $z'(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x)$. What is the function $Q(x)$?"] [17592186147331 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = x^3, \\qquad q(x) = 1, \\qquad n=-\\dfrac12\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by $y^{1/2}$, and we get\n\n$$\ny^{1/2} \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + x^3y^{3/2}= 1.\\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} = y^{3/2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3}{2}y^{1/2} \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y^{1/2}\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation $(\\ast)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^3\\,z = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{3}{2}x^3z = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nQ(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$"] [17592186147332 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{x^2}{y}$."] [17592186147332 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad q(x) = x^2, \\qquad n=-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by $y^{-1}$, and we get\n\n$$\ny \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + \\dfrac{y^{2}}{x}= x^2\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} = y^{2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{1}{x}z = x^2\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{2}{x} z =2x^2\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\frac{2}{x}, Q(x) = 2x^{2}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{∫P(x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{∫(2/x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{2\\ln x} \\\\\n&= x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{dz}{dx} + \\frac{2}{x}z) &= x^{2} \\cdot (2x^{2}) \\\\\nx^{2} \\cdot \\frac{dz}{dx} + 2xz &= 2x^{4} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(zx^{2}) &= 2x^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{d}{dx}(zx^{2})dx &= ∫2x^{4}dx \\\\\nzx^{2} &= \\frac{2x^{5}}{5} + C_{1} \\\\\nz &= \\frac{2x^{5} + C}{5x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5C_1$.\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z=y^{2}$. This results in the general solution\n\n$$\ny^{2} =\\dfrac{2x^5 + C}{5x^2}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186144017 17592186144018 17592186144019 17592186144020 17592186144021 17592186144023 17592186144024 17592186144025 1